  "final_text": "I created the dashboard and the alert rule, and the rule is enabled. It has already run once successfully and fired no alerts.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The data view `Kibana Sample Data Logs` uses `timestamp` as its time field. Each document is one web request. The main fields are:\n- **Request:** `clientip`, `url`/`request`, `extension`, `referer`, `agent`\n- **Response:** `response` (the HTTP status, stored as text with a `.keyword` version) and `bytes` (response size)\n- **Location:** `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`\n- **Client machine:** `machine.os`, `machine.ram`, plus `host` and `tags`\n\nAcross the whole index, response codes are 200 (12,832), 404 (801) and 503 (441).\n\n**2. Dashboard: `EFF-e8f8c89b Web logs overview`** (id `ba9166c4-2409-48c9-b56f-68d7ba826f3a`, default time range last 7 days). It has six panels:\n- Total requests\n- Unique visitors (distinct client IPs)\n- Average response size in bytes\n- Error rate: the share of 4xx and 5xx responses (8.82% across all data)\n- Requests over time as stacked bars, split by response code\n- Top 10 requested URLs\n\nI ran the queries behind the error-rate and top-URLs panels, plus a count by response code, directly against Kibana and they returned the expected results. I checked through the API that the dashboard is saved with all six panels, but I haven't opened it in the browser.\n\n**3. Rule: `EFF-e8f8c89b 5xx spike`** (id `2e6ce657-02a2-4282-a84e-4ca0f93e756d`). It's an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`:\n- It runs every minute.\n- It matches requests whose response code starts with 5 (the `response.keyword` field).\n- It fires when more than 10 of them arrive within 5 minutes, measured by `timestamp`.\n\nThe rule has no actions attached, so when it fires the alert only appears in Kibana; nobody is notified. If you want emails or other notifications, a connector needs to be added.\n\nThe request files I used are in the scratchpad as `dash.json` and `rule.json`.",
